wasi_stdlib.patch -- installed-provider repairs to the Zig 0.16 standard library

Target
  Apply with `git apply -p1` (or `patch -p1`) from a pristine Zig 0.16
  `<zig>/lib` root, then pass that directory to the compiler with the standard
  `--zig-lib-dir` option. No build-time download, custom SDK option, shim or global
  tool edit is involved: the copy is the installed `lib` tree with exactly the two hunks
  below applied, and no other standard library file differs from stock. It reproduces
  `providers/zig-wasi-stdlib/lib`.

Why
  A threaded libc-linked wasm32-wasi build (WASI SDK 34, WASIp1-threads, single_threaded=false
  on every module) failed to compile against stock Zig 0.16 (observed with Zig 0.16; not rechecked on 0.17).
  Neither hunk adds a capability, silences a diagnostic or substitutes a fake provider.

1. std/heap.zig -- threaded libc-linked wasm32-wasi page allocator.
   `page_allocator` selects the real `c_allocator` for the wasm32-wasi + libc +
   !single_threaded case, instead of instantiating `WasmAllocator`, which is
   `@compileError("unimplemented")` there. `c_allocator` is stock Zig code over
   the SDK's own thread-safe malloc/realloc/free and already satisfies the whole
   `Allocator` contract, including arbitrary alignment and OutOfMemory. (Zig's
   `have_posix_memalign` is false for `.wasi`, so on this target it uses its own
   manual-alignment strategy over that same real allocator.)
   The new branch also tests `builtin.os.tag == .wasi`, next to the wasm
   architecture, `builtin.link_libc` and `!builtin.single_threaded`, so it
   applies only to the wasm32-wasi build this provider exists for. No other
   target's behaviour changes: emscripten and every other non-`.wasi` wasm OS
   keep the upstream selection below even when threaded and libc-linked.
   Unchanged: the `root.os.heap.page_allocator` override, the single-threaded and
   non-libc WebAssembly `WasmAllocator` selection (so the "unimplemented"
   diagnostic still reports the genuinely unsupported threaded non-libc case),
   and every native `PageAllocator` path.
   `WasmAllocator` and the exported `wasm_allocator` are not touched; declarations
   are lazy, so the new path never instantiates `WasmAllocator`.

2. std/Io/Threaded.zig -- wasm `memory.atomic.wait32` timeout.
   A finite `timeout_ns` is clamped to `maxInt(i64)` before conversion, so it stays a
   finite non-negative nanosecond timeout in [0, 9223372036854775807] and can never wrap
   to a negative, i.e. indefinite, wait nor trap in `@intCast`. `null` still means "no
   timeout" and is still -1. Only the wasm branch of `Thread.futexWaitInner` changes: the
   `use_parking_futex` branch and every native OS branch stay byte-identical to stock, so
   native thread, park and timeout behaviour is unchanged.

Licensing
  This patch is fork-authored and touches only these two upstream Zig source
  files. It carries their original Std context, so the original copyright and
  license are retained: no upstream license, copyright or comment line is
  altered, no license is invented for this patch, and none is asserted beyond
  the unchanged upstream source context. The complete terms of the pinned
  compiler, copied verbatim, are in the sibling file
  `zig-0.16-LICENSE.txt` (MIT Expat, "Copyright (c) Zig contributors").

--- a/std/heap.zig
+++ b/std/heap.zig
@@ -342,6 +342,26 @@
     @hasDecl(root.os, "heap") and
     @hasDecl(root.os.heap, "page_allocator"))
     root.os.heap.page_allocator
+// foundation-libs provider change: threaded wasm32-wasi linked against a real
+// libc (built with the WASIp1-threads sysroot) has no working `memory.grow`
+// page allocator, and upstream `WasmAllocator` is
+// `@compileError("unimplemented")` for `!builtin.single_threaded`, so selecting
+// it here is a hard compile failure rather than a merely weaker allocator.
+// Use the real thread-safe libc allocator instead: `c_allocator` already
+// implements the complete `Allocator` contract (malloc/posix_memalign or manual
+// alignment, resize, remap, free) over the SDK's own allocator. No new
+// allocation layer, simulated mapping, or capability claim is added, and the
+// `root.os.heap.page_allocator` override above still takes precedence.
+//
+// Every other WebAssembly target, and wasm32-wasi without libc or when
+// single-threaded, keeps the upstream selection below, so the "unimplemented"
+// diagnostic still honestly reports the genuinely unsupported threaded
+// non-libc case. `WasmAllocator` is a lazy declaration, so it is not analyzed
+// by this path at all.
+else if (builtin.target.cpu.arch.isWasm() and
+    builtin.os.tag == .wasi and
+    builtin.link_libc and
+    !builtin.single_threaded) c_allocator
 else if (builtin.target.cpu.arch.isWasm()) .{
     .ptr = undefined,
     .vtable = &WasmAllocator.vtable,
--- a/std/Io/Threaded.zig
+++ b/std/Io/Threaded.zig
@@ -956,7 +956,18 @@
             comptime assert(builtin.cpu.has(.wasm, .atomics));
             // TODO implement cancelation for WASM futex waits by signaling the futex
             if (!uncancelable) try Thread.checkCancel();
-            const to: i64 = if (timeout_ns) |ns| ns else -1;
+            // foundation-libs provider change: `memory.atomic.wait32` takes an
+            // i64 nanosecond timeout, and any negative value means "wait
+            // indefinitely". The upstream line here assigns the u64 `ns`
+            // straight to an i64, which does not compile for a threaded wasm
+            // target. Saturate instead: a finite timeout can then never wrap
+            // to a negative (indefinite) wait, and `@intCast` cannot trap on
+            // an absurdly large duration. `null` still means "no timeout" and
+            // stays -1. Native branches are untouched.
+            const to: i64 = if (timeout_ns) |ns|
+                @intCast(@min(ns, @as(u64, std.math.maxInt(i64))))
+            else
+                -1;
             const signed_expect: i32 = @bitCast(expect);
             const result = asm volatile (
                 \\local.get %[ptr]
